00:05:13.090 --> 00:05:14.740
a manager on the DevRel team.

00:05:14.740 --> 00:05:16.439
And Mandy, thanks
for joining us.

00:05:16.439 --> 00:05:17.980
MANDY WAITE: Thank
you for having me.

00:05:17.980 --> 00:05:19.960
MELANIE WARRICK: I'm glad our
schedules finally aligned.

00:05:19.960 --> 00:05:21.100
Now that we have
you here, can you

00:05:21.100 --> 00:05:22.540
tell the people who are
listening a little bit

00:05:22.540 --> 00:05:24.140
about who you are, what you do?

00:05:24.140 --> 00:05:26.223
MANDY WAITE: So my
name is Mandy Waite.

00:05:26.223 --> 00:05:28.180
I am Developer Advocate.

00:05:28.180 --> 00:05:30.160
I'm not actually a
manager of that currently.

00:05:30.160 --> 00:05:32.650
I'm a Developer Advocate
at Google Cloud.

00:05:32.650 --> 00:05:36.940
I'm on a team of infrastructure
and operations advocates,

00:05:36.940 --> 00:05:40.450
working with a wide variety of
communities, such as DevOps,

00:05:40.450 --> 00:05:42.910
SRE, Cloud-native,
Cloud-native Ops--

00:05:42.910 --> 00:05:45.297
potentially Security and
Networking in the future.

00:05:45.297 --> 00:05:46.880
We're still growing
the team, so we're

00:05:46.880 --> 00:05:50.050
focused very much on DevOps
and Cloud-native currently.

00:05:50.050 --> 00:05:54.070
I've been around Google
for nearly eight years now.

00:05:54.070 --> 00:05:55.810
I came from Sun
Microsystems where

00:05:55.810 --> 00:05:57.910
I used to do a similar
role, but which

00:05:57.910 --> 00:05:59.980
was much more marketing
aligned, trying to get

00:05:59.980 --> 00:06:01.780
people to adopt our stuff.

00:06:01.780 --> 00:06:04.810
And I got a job with
Google working on AdWords

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:06.400
as a Developer Advocate.

00:06:06.400 --> 00:06:08.920
That was interesting,
an extremely great

00:06:08.920 --> 00:06:11.500
technical challenge, because
the API, the AdWords API,

00:06:11.500 --> 00:06:13.090
is extremely complicated.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:14.240
And it's a lot of fun.

00:06:14.240 --> 00:06:15.070
And it has a scale.

00:06:15.070 --> 00:06:17.590
And understanding that was
really, really useful to me.

00:06:17.590 --> 00:06:20.770
The kind of background I
had was scaling systems,

00:06:20.770 --> 00:06:22.720
and scaling APIs was new to me.

00:06:22.720 --> 00:06:24.970
But it had very much the
same kind of similar problems

00:06:24.970 --> 00:06:27.235
that I've been facing
in the past with Sun.

00:06:27.235 --> 00:06:30.610
And then I moved to Cloud
in 2012, at the end of 2012.

00:06:30.610 --> 00:06:32.320
And I've been on
Cloud ever since.

00:06:32.320 --> 00:06:35.770
And we've grown from a small
team of people, a mix of roles

00:06:35.770 --> 00:06:38.435
before, to a small team
of Developer Advocates,

00:06:38.435 --> 00:06:41.140
to a team of a number
of areas over time.

00:06:41.140 --> 00:06:42.430
And I think it's 46.

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:44.110
So we have a large team now.

00:06:44.110 --> 00:06:46.620
We generally no longer call
ourselves Developer Advocates.

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:48.370
We've much more
aligned with being

00:06:48.370 --> 00:06:50.070
advocates, or Cloud Advocates.

00:06:50.070 --> 00:06:52.630
On a day, we do Developer
Advocacy as well.

00:06:52.630 --> 00:06:56.790
For me, it's much more around
not really covering developers

00:06:56.790 --> 00:06:57.290
anymore.

00:06:57.290 --> 00:06:59.020
It's more about talking
to other communities,

00:06:59.020 --> 00:07:01.180
and ones that are very,
very important to us when

00:07:01.180 --> 00:07:03.280
it comes to making sure
that we have the best

00:07:03.280 --> 00:07:04.240
possible products.

00:07:04.240 --> 00:07:06.970
And that would be the DevOps
community, Cloud-native,

00:07:06.970 --> 00:07:08.550
Cloud-native
Operations communities.

00:07:08.550 --> 00:07:09.960
SRE is a great story.

00:07:09.960 --> 00:07:12.730
Lots of people are starting
to look at doing SRE--

00:07:12.730 --> 00:07:14.290
security for the
future, probably

00:07:14.290 --> 00:07:17.170
security across the board,
across all different aspects

00:07:17.170 --> 00:07:18.860
of technology.

00:07:18.860 --> 00:07:20.497
So that's what we're
doing currently.

00:07:20.497 --> 00:07:21.580
As I say, I manage a team.

00:07:21.580 --> 00:07:23.629
I have about 10 people
in my team, currently.

00:07:23.629 --> 00:07:25.420
MELANIE WARRICK: And
your team focuses on--

00:07:25.420 --> 00:07:27.760
you were saying-- you're on
infrastructure and operations,

00:07:27.760 --> 00:07:28.090
right?

00:07:28.090 --> 00:07:29.560
MANDY WAITE: Infrastructure,
operations, yeah.

00:07:29.560 --> 00:07:30.670
It's a very general term.

00:07:30.670 --> 00:07:32.128
I'm not sure if
it's the best term,

00:07:32.128 --> 00:07:33.742
but it's the one we landed on.

00:07:33.742 --> 00:07:35.950
I originally called it
Operations and Infrastructure.

00:07:35.950 --> 00:07:38.260
But somehow, it
got turned around.

00:07:38.260 --> 00:07:40.510
I'm not sure why, but I don't
think it really matters.

00:07:40.510 --> 00:07:42.700
It could be the DevOps team or
the DevOps Cloud-native team,

00:07:42.700 --> 00:07:44.230
but Infrastructure
and Operations

00:07:44.230 --> 00:07:45.300
seems to cover it more.

00:07:45.300 --> 00:07:47.020
It's definitely not
developer-centric.

00:07:47.020 --> 00:07:49.520
MARK MANDEL: So I'm going to
ask you the hard question then.

00:07:49.520 --> 00:07:50.240
MANDY WAITE: OK.

00:07:50.240 --> 00:07:52.650
MARK MANDEL: How do you
define developer relations?

00:07:52.650 --> 00:07:53.400
MANDY WAITE: Yeah.

00:07:53.400 --> 00:07:54.910
So interesting question.

00:07:54.910 --> 00:07:56.649
To be honest, I mean,
the fact that we

00:07:56.649 --> 00:07:58.690
work with other communities
other than developers

00:07:58.690 --> 00:08:00.106
gives us an
interesting challenge,

00:08:00.106 --> 00:08:02.530
because some
umbrella term that we

00:08:02.530 --> 00:08:04.870
can use to reference
all of these communities

00:08:04.870 --> 00:08:05.750
that we work with--

00:08:05.750 --> 00:08:08.830
developer community, the DevOps
community, the Data Science

00:08:08.830 --> 00:08:11.470
community-- there is no
really good umbrella term.

00:08:11.470 --> 00:08:14.190
And we tend to fall back
on technical communities

00:08:14.190 --> 00:08:16.424
as being the umbrella term,
but it's not a good one.

00:08:16.424 --> 00:08:18.340
We're still fundamentally
developer relations,

00:08:18.340 --> 00:08:21.800
but we've just really kind of
overloaded the term "developer"

00:08:21.800 --> 00:08:24.140
to mean all of these
other communities as well.

00:08:24.140 --> 00:08:26.989
And so Developer Relations
is actually making sure

00:08:26.989 --> 00:08:29.530
that we have the best possible
products and the best possible

00:08:29.530 --> 00:08:32.320
experience for people using
the products as possible

00:08:32.320 --> 00:08:37.299
and also making sure that
we communicate what we have,

00:08:37.299 --> 00:08:39.309
take our narratives and
our messages on the road

00:08:39.309 --> 00:08:41.407
and show them to
people, help them

00:08:41.407 --> 00:08:43.240
understand how they can
solve their problems

00:08:43.240 --> 00:08:44.290
with our products.

00:08:44.290 --> 00:08:46.000
Because that's
extremely important.

00:08:46.000 --> 00:08:48.950
But not forcing products down
people's throats and saying,

00:08:48.950 --> 00:08:50.560
hey, we have this thing.

00:08:50.560 --> 00:08:51.400
You should use this.

00:08:51.400 --> 00:08:53.358
It's not around giving
people product pictures,

00:08:53.358 --> 00:08:56.290
it's about talking
about how we can solve

00:08:56.290 --> 00:08:58.391
problems using our technology.

00:08:58.391 --> 00:09:00.140
And often, that's a
case of meeting people

00:09:00.140 --> 00:09:01.690
where they are when you
have something like Cloud,

00:09:01.690 --> 00:09:03.580
because that involves
using tooling

00:09:03.580 --> 00:09:06.170
that they're familiar with
already with our Cloud product.

00:09:06.170 --> 00:09:08.230
So if they're using
something like Jenkins

00:09:08.230 --> 00:09:10.470
to do build pipelines
and such, like CI/CD,

00:09:10.470 --> 00:09:12.945
then we want Jenkins
to work really well.

00:09:12.945 --> 00:09:15.070
And a great example of that
would be Infrastructure

00:09:15.070 --> 00:09:16.524
as Code, that kind of thing--

00:09:16.524 --> 00:09:18.190
having people making
sure they use Share

00:09:18.190 --> 00:09:19.740
from Puppet and Terraform--

00:09:19.740 --> 00:09:22.150
and that those things
work perfectly well

00:09:22.150 --> 00:09:23.960
with our Cloud Platform.

00:09:23.960 --> 00:09:26.710
There is also an aspect of
being opinionated as well--

00:09:26.710 --> 00:09:28.689
say like, Google
do things this way.

00:09:28.689 --> 00:09:30.730
And this is what we've
been doing with Kubernetes

00:09:30.730 --> 00:09:31.420
for a long time.

00:09:31.420 --> 00:09:33.719
So four years ago, we
saw this with Kubernetes.

00:09:33.719 --> 00:09:35.260
It was new to us,
apart from the fact

00:09:35.260 --> 00:09:37.690
that we knew how things
worked internally

00:09:37.690 --> 00:09:38.622
at Google, with Borg.

00:09:38.622 --> 00:09:41.080
And I think you've had John
[? Wilkes ?] talking about Borg

00:09:41.080 --> 00:09:42.147
before.

00:09:42.147 --> 00:09:44.230
Borg was really, really
interesting to many of us.

00:09:44.230 --> 00:09:46.521
And taking that message out
on the road to show people,

00:09:46.521 --> 00:09:48.650
hey, have you ever
considered doing it this way?

00:09:48.650 --> 00:09:50.980
It was in the back end of
the container revolution.

00:09:50.980 --> 00:09:53.349
And my message back
then was very much, hey,

00:09:53.349 --> 00:09:54.640
I have all of these containers.

00:09:54.640 --> 00:09:55.900
Now what do I do with them?

00:09:55.900 --> 00:09:58.730
And Kubernets was
the solution to that.

00:09:58.730 --> 00:10:01.590
It was highly opinionated,
but it was also a new paradigm

00:10:01.590 --> 00:10:02.240
as well.

00:10:02.240 --> 00:10:04.150
And I think people have
adopted it at scale.

00:10:04.150 --> 00:10:07.540
And I think it's a really
good inflection curve showing

00:10:07.540 --> 00:10:09.280
adoption of Kubernetes.

00:10:09.280 --> 00:10:11.890
And I'd like to see
more of that happening.

00:10:11.890 --> 00:10:13.479
But being opinionated
about something,

00:10:13.479 --> 00:10:14.770
you have to be careful with it.

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:16.450
Because you can't
always change the way

00:10:16.450 --> 00:10:18.141
people feel about something.

00:10:18.141 --> 00:10:20.390
Meeting people where they
are is very, very important.

00:10:20.390 --> 00:10:22.480
But if you have a
better way, then you

00:10:22.480 --> 00:10:24.880
should be showing them
how they can be successful

00:10:24.880 --> 00:10:27.486
doing it that way,
but without dismissing

00:10:27.486 --> 00:10:29.110
what they're doing
currently and making

00:10:29.110 --> 00:10:31.202
it seem like what they're
doing may be trivial

00:10:31.202 --> 00:10:32.410
compared to what we're doing.

00:10:32.410 --> 00:10:34.494
"Build it like Google"
might be a great message,

00:10:34.494 --> 00:10:36.910
but we have to be respectful
of the fact that people build

00:10:36.910 --> 00:10:38.202
things their own way currently.

00:10:38.202 --> 00:10:40.535
And if this helps them and
solves some of their problems

00:10:40.535 --> 00:10:41.830
for them, then that's great.

00:10:41.830 --> 00:10:42.970
And we can take
those messages out,

00:10:42.970 --> 00:10:44.261
and people will listen to them.

00:10:44.261 --> 00:10:46.000
But we have to meet
people where they are

00:10:46.000 --> 00:10:49.466
and keep your opinionation
a little bit more lower key.

00:10:49.466 --> 00:10:51.340
MELANIE WARRICK: So you
were saying about how

00:10:51.340 --> 00:10:52.750
this being Developer Relations.

00:10:52.750 --> 00:10:54.590
What comprises a
Developer Relations?

00:10:54.590 --> 00:10:56.590
MANDY WAITE: What comprises
Developer Relations?

00:10:56.590 --> 00:10:58.340
I got people.

00:10:58.340 --> 00:10:58.840
[LAUGHTER]

00:10:58.840 --> 00:11:01.131
MELANIE WARRICK: Well, so
part of the reason I'm asking

00:11:01.131 --> 00:11:03.520
is Mark and I are officially
Developer Advocates.

00:11:03.520 --> 00:11:05.590
And I know we have a
number of different roles

00:11:05.590 --> 00:11:06.340
within the team.

00:11:06.340 --> 00:11:08.260
And so I'm basically
setting this up

00:11:08.260 --> 00:11:10.480
to say, hey, what are
some of the roles, what

00:11:10.480 --> 00:11:13.100
are some of the makeup of
the teams that we have?

00:11:13.100 --> 00:11:14.225
MANDY WAITE: Yeah.

00:11:14.225 --> 00:11:15.180
I don't think I
was being flippant

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:16.888
by saying people as
well, because I think

00:11:16.888 --> 00:11:19.060
it's very important that
the people aspect of it

00:11:19.060 --> 00:11:21.460
is so massively
important to this role.

00:11:21.460 --> 00:11:23.322
It is very much about
working with people

00:11:23.322 --> 00:11:25.030
and being to communicate
with them, to be

00:11:25.030 --> 00:11:27.270
able to understand their needs.

00:11:27.270 --> 00:11:30.070
Empathy is a huge word,
a word that we use a lot.

00:11:30.070 --> 00:11:32.140
Another word that is
really important is trust.

00:11:32.140 --> 00:11:34.150
So trust and empathy are
very much people things

00:11:34.150 --> 00:11:36.705
and not related to technology
in any meaningful way.

00:11:36.705 --> 00:11:39.610
And I think it's probably
very easy to be in technology

00:11:39.610 --> 00:11:42.610
and not have a lot of trust
and not have a lot of empathy.

00:11:42.610 --> 00:11:45.160
And I think one of
the keys to being

00:11:45.160 --> 00:11:46.720
successful in
Developer Relations

00:11:46.720 --> 00:11:49.690
is being able to
have those skills--

00:11:49.690 --> 00:11:50.900
not necessarily develop them.

00:11:50.900 --> 00:11:52.645
I think you can develop empathy.

00:11:52.645 --> 00:11:54.580
I always go back
to something I saw

00:11:54.580 --> 00:11:56.560
when I was working with
a TV rental company

00:11:56.560 --> 00:11:59.680
back in the UK a long, long time
ago, when I was 16 years old.

00:11:59.680 --> 00:12:01.870
And they used to have
a sign on the wall

00:12:01.870 --> 00:12:04.710
next to the reception
area saying, "Be Sincere."

00:12:04.710 --> 00:12:07.040
And I've always said,
you can't be sincere.

00:12:07.040 --> 00:12:08.610
You either are or you aren't.

00:12:08.610 --> 00:12:11.050
And I think there's some
of that with empathy.

00:12:11.050 --> 00:12:14.110
Empathy is something you
potentially can develop,

00:12:14.110 --> 00:12:15.190
but it is deep.

00:12:15.190 --> 00:12:16.540
It is fundamental.

00:12:16.540 --> 00:12:18.790
You can't make people think
you have empathy for them.

00:12:18.790 --> 00:12:20.637
You either do or you don't.

00:12:20.637 --> 00:12:22.720
And I think having empathy
is something that I see

00:12:22.720 --> 00:12:24.580
across the whole of the team.

00:12:24.580 --> 00:12:26.704
And I think that's really,
really important for us.

00:12:26.704 --> 00:12:28.730
And the trust part of it
is also implicit, also.

00:12:28.730 --> 00:12:31.270
MARK MANDEL: So what benefits
does Google Cloud get out

00:12:31.270 --> 00:12:33.790
of the relationship that it has
with its Developer Relations

00:12:33.790 --> 00:12:34.289
team?

00:12:34.289 --> 00:12:35.292
Why do we even exist?

00:12:35.292 --> 00:12:36.000
MANDY WAITE: Yes.

00:12:36.000 --> 00:12:39.539
I began 20 years,
probably, doing this work.

00:12:39.539 --> 00:12:41.830
People have always known we
were very important and not

00:12:41.830 --> 00:12:45.010
really quite known why
or been prepared to take

00:12:45.010 --> 00:12:47.440
the risk of not having DevRel.

00:12:47.440 --> 00:12:49.390
Back at Sun
Microsystems, we used

00:12:49.390 --> 00:12:51.670
to be moved from
software organizations

00:12:51.670 --> 00:12:53.410
to marketing organizations
because people

00:12:53.410 --> 00:12:55.930
knew that we were absolutely
necessary, but not quite sure

00:12:55.930 --> 00:12:58.600
where we fitted into
the organization.

00:12:58.600 --> 00:13:01.270
So why do we have DevRel?

00:13:01.270 --> 00:13:02.770
Well, I actually
asked this question

00:13:02.770 --> 00:13:04.790
to some people a
couple of years ago.

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:07.420
We were doing some
strategizing around things.

00:13:07.420 --> 00:13:08.990
And I asked the
question, what would

00:13:08.990 --> 00:13:10.240
happen if there was no DevRel?

00:13:10.240 --> 00:13:12.430
And the answer was very much,
we would have worse products.

00:13:12.430 --> 00:13:14.180
We would have worse
developer experiences.

00:13:14.180 --> 00:13:17.620
And I said, I thought the answer
was that the product teams

00:13:17.620 --> 00:13:19.630
would create their own DevRel.

00:13:19.630 --> 00:13:23.410
It was an essential
role within the team.

00:13:23.410 --> 00:13:24.910
We would need a
product team to have

00:13:24.910 --> 00:13:26.770
some kind of DevRel function.

00:13:26.770 --> 00:13:29.354
Some PMs, many PMs,
thought that they

00:13:29.354 --> 00:13:31.270
have a good handle on
what customers are doing

00:13:31.270 --> 00:13:32.410
and such like.

00:13:32.410 --> 00:13:35.110
Not all of them feel very
close to their actual community

00:13:35.110 --> 00:13:35.720
of users.

00:13:35.720 --> 00:13:37.553
Could be, in this case,
the DevOps community

00:13:37.553 --> 00:13:39.420
in my team, or the
developer community.

00:13:39.420 --> 00:13:40.945
And they know that they
need to go out and get

00:13:40.945 --> 00:13:41.720
that information.

00:13:41.720 --> 00:13:42.850
They also need to
know that they need

00:13:42.850 --> 00:13:44.460
to take their messages
out and show people

00:13:44.460 --> 00:13:46.376
how they can be successful
with those products

00:13:46.376 --> 00:13:47.450
that they're building.

00:13:47.450 --> 00:13:51.010
So they would fundamentally
build their own DevRel team.

00:13:51.010 --> 00:13:53.350
And if you look at that
scale across a large number

00:13:53.350 --> 00:13:55.308
of products-- and going
back to when I started,

00:13:55.308 --> 00:13:59.440
it was things like AdWords,
Web, Chrome, Android--

00:13:59.440 --> 00:14:02.470
you would have lots of different
DevRel functions growing up

00:14:02.470 --> 00:14:03.290
in parallel.

00:14:03.290 --> 00:14:06.029
And commonalities,
scale, it would

00:14:06.029 --> 00:14:07.570
make it sensible to
pull them all out

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:09.460
and put them together
in one place.

00:14:09.460 --> 00:14:11.650
I would argue that
there are always

00:14:11.650 --> 00:14:14.410
problems around doing
DevRel in a cookie cutter

00:14:14.410 --> 00:14:16.090
way for a specific product.

00:14:16.090 --> 00:14:18.659
It needs to be different in
kind for each different product.

00:14:18.659 --> 00:14:20.700
I think that's extremely
important to understand.

00:14:20.700 --> 00:14:22.300
And it also depends
where a product

00:14:22.300 --> 00:14:23.780
is in its product lifecycle.

00:14:23.780 --> 00:14:25.280
It depends on your
audience as well.

00:14:25.280 --> 00:14:27.310
So those considerations
are important.

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:29.140
But I think having
the DevRel team,

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:31.480
we can benefit from
large scale programs.

00:14:31.480 --> 00:14:35.560
The kind of mentality and the
mindset of people that we hire

00:14:35.560 --> 00:14:36.979
gives us a huge
edge when it comes

00:14:36.979 --> 00:14:39.520
to doing DevRel, as opposed to
having a product team building

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:40.790
their own team.

00:14:40.790 --> 00:14:43.150
So I think that's why we
have DevRel currently.

00:14:43.150 --> 00:14:43.650
Yeah.

00:14:43.650 --> 00:14:44.770
I think I'll leave
it at that because I

00:14:44.770 --> 00:14:46.570
know one of the next questions
you're going to ask me about,

00:14:46.570 --> 00:14:48.370
how we measure it, which
is going to be interesting.

00:14:48.370 --> 00:14:50.661
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah, well,
OK, how do you measure it?

00:14:50.661 --> 00:14:53.054
What are ways that you can
determine it's impactful?

00:14:53.054 --> 00:14:54.970
MANDY WAITE: I've given
this a lot of thought,

00:14:54.970 --> 00:14:56.650
a lot of thought over the years.

00:14:56.650 --> 00:14:59.836
I think there is always
consequences of the work

00:14:59.836 --> 00:15:01.210
that we do-- the
number of people

00:15:01.210 --> 00:15:02.710
who watch a video,
the length of time

00:15:02.710 --> 00:15:05.020
that they watch the video,
the amount of engagement they

00:15:05.020 --> 00:15:06.400
have with the things
that we produce.

00:15:06.400 --> 00:15:08.070
And they could be looking
at a meeting post, or even

00:15:08.070 --> 00:15:09.050
a blog post.

00:15:09.050 --> 00:15:12.100
Often, those things are very
easy to measure and capture.

00:15:12.100 --> 00:15:14.800
I think there is a danger
around having a goal

00:15:14.800 --> 00:15:16.556
around those numbers, always.

00:15:16.556 --> 00:15:19.180
I don't think it's a good thing
to have a goal around a number,

00:15:19.180 --> 00:15:24.450
to say we should have 1 million
video views within Q1 or Q2.

00:15:24.450 --> 00:15:26.920
I don't think that's a
very good way of doing it,

00:15:26.920 --> 00:15:28.400
but it is a good metric.

00:15:28.400 --> 00:15:30.150
And so if you look at
the work that you do

00:15:30.150 --> 00:15:32.110
and the things that
you do, some metrics

00:15:32.110 --> 00:15:33.130
are going to bubble out of that.

00:15:33.130 --> 00:15:34.588
And you can push
them up to people.

00:15:34.588 --> 00:15:36.942
But they shouldn't be the
reason why you're doing it.

00:15:36.942 --> 00:15:38.650
So the reason you
would do a video series

00:15:38.650 --> 00:15:40.960
was to get important
information out to people

00:15:40.960 --> 00:15:43.690
or to drive some strategic
goal that you really care about

00:15:43.690 --> 00:15:45.730
and that you've agreed
with the product teams.

00:15:45.730 --> 00:15:48.231
And ultimately, the metrics
will drop out of that.

00:15:48.231 --> 00:15:49.480
And you can report up on them.

00:15:49.480 --> 00:15:53.080
And you can say, we did 500
events or 600 events last year.

00:15:53.080 --> 00:15:55.990
We did 150 videos, and
we had x number of views,

00:15:55.990 --> 00:15:58.949
and x number of in-person
outreach was done.

00:15:58.949 --> 00:16:00.490
But I don't think
they're the numbers

00:16:00.490 --> 00:16:02.080
that we should be driving.

00:16:02.080 --> 00:16:03.149
I think one thing--

00:16:03.149 --> 00:16:04.690
this is a little
bit dangerous, but I

00:16:04.690 --> 00:16:07.690
think it's interesting to
think about this more-- is

00:16:07.690 --> 00:16:11.290
that products tend to
have a graph of adoption,

00:16:11.290 --> 00:16:15.370
or usage, utilization,
or efficacy, usefulness,

00:16:15.370 --> 00:16:16.550
and that kind of thing.

00:16:16.550 --> 00:16:18.590
And those graphs-- you
naturally want them

00:16:18.590 --> 00:16:19.879
to go off and to the right.

00:16:19.879 --> 00:16:20.795
Probably not linearly.

00:16:20.795 --> 00:16:23.450
You'd like exponential
growth, if possible.

00:16:23.450 --> 00:16:25.784
But I think your products are
going up and to the right,

00:16:25.784 --> 00:16:27.325
and you have a DevRel
function there,

00:16:27.325 --> 00:16:29.650
I think that shows that your
DevRel function is working

00:16:29.650 --> 00:16:31.275
and doing things correctly.

00:16:31.275 --> 00:16:32.900
You might be tempted
to say, well, just

00:16:32.900 --> 00:16:35.184
take my DevRel function
away and see if it flattens.

00:16:35.184 --> 00:16:36.850
But you're not going
to want to do that.

00:16:36.850 --> 00:16:38.141
So there is always that danger.

00:16:38.141 --> 00:16:39.460
You're not quite sure.

00:16:39.460 --> 00:16:41.450
You can't necessarily
attribute the work

00:16:41.450 --> 00:16:44.890
that DevRel does to go up
and to your right curve.

00:16:44.890 --> 00:16:47.050
But I think it's
important to understand

00:16:47.050 --> 00:16:49.660
it is a fundamental part of
all of your process of building

00:16:49.660 --> 00:16:51.424
a product and
bringing it to market,

00:16:51.424 --> 00:16:53.590
is that you have a DevRel
function that's ultimately

00:16:53.590 --> 00:16:55.720
driving the product to be
better, based on people's

00:16:55.720 --> 00:16:57.970
feedback, and that you're
showing people how to use it

00:16:57.970 --> 00:16:59.979
and making sure people
are aware of it.

00:16:59.979 --> 00:17:02.020
I think awareness is a
great thing, but awareness

00:17:02.020 --> 00:17:05.079
is so difficult.
It's so difficult.

00:17:05.079 --> 00:17:07.270
It still seems to
me that there's

00:17:07.270 --> 00:17:09.016
a large number of
people out there,

00:17:09.016 --> 00:17:10.849
a large percentage of
people-- not a number,

00:17:10.849 --> 00:17:12.880
a large percentage of people
there-- that have not heard

00:17:12.880 --> 00:17:14.046
about Google Cloud products.

00:17:14.046 --> 00:17:16.615
And that seems strange,
considering the amount of work

00:17:16.615 --> 00:17:17.430
that we've done.

00:17:17.430 --> 00:17:20.010
But I just think that there
are a large number of people

00:17:20.010 --> 00:17:20.510
out there.

00:17:20.510 --> 00:17:22.176
And I think we have
to be aware of that.

00:17:22.176 --> 00:17:23.857
And a lot of people
work in environments

00:17:23.857 --> 00:17:26.440
where they don't get exposed to
all of these things very much,

00:17:26.440 --> 00:17:29.420
and so maybe they don't
know of it because of that.

00:17:29.420 --> 00:17:31.600
I'd like everybody to
know about our products

00:17:31.600 --> 00:17:34.150
and how to be successful
with those products.

00:17:34.150 --> 00:17:36.070
I'd like to be getting
feedback from everybody

00:17:36.070 --> 00:17:38.200
so that we can bring that
back to the product teams

00:17:38.200 --> 00:17:39.790
and make our products better.

00:17:39.790 --> 00:17:41.720
I don't know how
we can reach that,

00:17:41.720 --> 00:17:44.170
but I think we probably need
to continue growing DevRel

00:17:44.170 --> 00:17:45.622
as the product teams grow.

00:17:45.622 --> 00:17:47.080
I think we lag
behind a little bit.

00:17:47.080 --> 00:17:49.564
As the products grow,
DevRel is always

00:17:49.564 --> 00:17:50.980
a little bit behind
the curve when

00:17:50.980 --> 00:17:53.300
it comes to adding more people.

00:17:53.300 --> 00:17:55.400
MELANIE WARRICK: So
in terms of impact,

00:17:55.400 --> 00:17:57.590
what are some of the
activities you've seen

00:17:57.590 --> 00:17:59.239
that have had the most impact?

00:17:59.239 --> 00:18:01.030
MANDY WAITE: That's a
really good question.

00:18:01.030 --> 00:18:02.726
MELANIE WARRICK: And you
didn't give that one to me.

00:18:02.726 --> 00:18:03.225
[LAUGHTER]

00:18:03.225 --> 00:18:04.481
MANDY WAITE: You're right.

00:18:04.481 --> 00:18:05.480
That is a good question.

00:18:05.480 --> 00:18:08.390
And I think people do
things in different ways.

00:18:08.390 --> 00:18:10.587
I think the great thing
about our DevRel team

00:18:10.587 --> 00:18:12.670
is there is no cookie
cutter approach to doing it.

00:18:12.670 --> 00:18:13.641
We do it differently.

00:18:13.641 --> 00:18:15.890
We do it based on our own
skills, our own experiences,

00:18:15.890 --> 00:18:17.450
the things that we're good at.

00:18:17.450 --> 00:18:19.900
We do it based on our own
products in our own communities

00:18:19.900 --> 00:18:22.358
that we're trying to reach and
our own goals and strategies

00:18:22.358 --> 00:18:23.120
as well.

00:18:23.120 --> 00:18:24.390
So I see lots of
different things and lots

00:18:24.390 --> 00:18:25.750
of different success stories.

00:18:25.750 --> 00:18:28.135
I think having a
podcast is a great idea.

00:18:28.135 --> 00:18:28.420
MELANIE WARRICK: Thank you.

00:18:28.420 --> 00:18:28.610
MARK MANDEL: Thanks.

00:18:28.610 --> 00:18:31.220
MANDY WAITE: So whoever came
up with that idea should be--

00:18:31.220 --> 00:18:31.740
MELANIE WARRICK:
It was all Mark.

00:18:31.740 --> 00:18:33.010
MARK MANDEL: I have
no idea who that was.

00:18:33.010 --> 00:18:33.820
MELANIE WARRICK:
Maybe it was your dog.

00:18:33.820 --> 00:18:34.360
[DOG BARKS]

00:18:34.360 --> 00:18:35.140
MARK MANDEL: It was
Francisco's idea.

00:18:35.140 --> 00:18:35.770
I'll give it to him.

00:18:35.770 --> 00:18:37.853
MANDY WAITE: But we got
very good at doing videos.

00:18:37.853 --> 00:18:39.400
And we felt in
the past two years

00:18:39.400 --> 00:18:40.750
ago that we weren't very
good at doing videos.

00:18:40.750 --> 00:18:42.430
And now we're much
better at doing videos.

00:18:42.430 --> 00:18:44.221
We get a lot of attention
from our videos--

00:18:44.221 --> 00:18:46.811
very short, very succinct,
and very to-the-point.

00:18:46.811 --> 00:18:49.060
We socialize them very well,
and we get lots of views.

00:18:49.060 --> 00:18:50.560
And I think that's
really important.

00:18:50.560 --> 00:18:51.820
That's really impactful.

00:18:51.820 --> 00:18:53.800
I think that for
open source projects,

00:18:53.800 --> 00:18:56.860
which is really a new thing
around doing advocacy for--

00:18:56.860 --> 00:19:00.100
or DevRel for, generally,
I won't singular advocacy,

00:19:00.100 --> 00:19:03.190
but just DevRel generally--
is that it's a different kind

00:19:03.190 --> 00:19:04.435
of way of doing things.

00:19:04.435 --> 00:19:06.310
And I look at somebody
like [? Yonda Doken ?]

00:19:06.310 --> 00:19:08.290
on my team, who's
working on open sensors,

00:19:08.290 --> 00:19:11.510
and she's really involved
in the community.

00:19:11.510 --> 00:19:13.160
She was a [INAUDIBLE]
on the community.

00:19:13.160 --> 00:19:15.020
She's been doing lots of
contributions to that.

00:19:15.020 --> 00:19:16.660
But she understands
exactly what the mission is

00:19:16.660 --> 00:19:17.350
and what the goal is.

00:19:17.350 --> 00:19:19.270
And she understands the
community and their needs.

00:19:19.270 --> 00:19:21.560
And she understands what she
would like to get out of it.

00:19:21.560 --> 00:19:23.020
And she's doing a
different kind of DevRel

00:19:23.020 --> 00:19:24.230
there with open sensors.

00:19:24.230 --> 00:19:26.021
So that's another
different way of doing it

00:19:26.021 --> 00:19:28.020
and another different
way of being successful.

00:19:28.020 --> 00:19:30.760
So I think it's really a
case of constantly exploring

00:19:30.760 --> 00:19:32.200
and constantly adapting.

00:19:32.200 --> 00:19:33.010
I have this goal.

00:19:33.010 --> 00:19:34.510
I want to achieve
this, which may be

00:19:34.510 --> 00:19:36.812
I want to grow
adoption of BigQuery--

00:19:36.812 --> 00:19:38.770
constantly thinking about
new ways of doing it.

00:19:38.770 --> 00:19:41.170
Maybe going on Reddit and
getting millions and billions

00:19:41.170 --> 00:19:43.450
of people to actually talk
about your product, which

00:19:43.450 --> 00:19:44.534
somebody on our team does.

00:19:44.534 --> 00:19:45.449
MARK MANDEL: Philippe.

00:19:45.449 --> 00:19:46.810
MANDY WAITE: Yeah, Philippe.

00:19:46.810 --> 00:19:49.476
So yeah, so I think there's lots
of great things that people do.

00:19:49.476 --> 00:19:51.580
And I'm just hoping that
there's more innovation.

00:19:51.580 --> 00:19:53.950
Because you look at
it, and you think,

00:19:53.950 --> 00:19:55.450
we've done all of
the DevRel we can,

00:19:55.450 --> 00:19:56.740
all the different
ways of doing DevRel.

00:19:56.740 --> 00:19:59.073
But then somebody's going to
come up with something new.

00:19:59.073 --> 00:20:01.810
Oh, actually [INAUDIBLE] is
currently doing live stream

00:20:01.810 --> 00:20:02.560
coding--

00:20:02.560 --> 00:20:04.300
Twitch for DevRel, effectively.

00:20:04.300 --> 00:20:05.590
And I really like that.

00:20:05.590 --> 00:20:07.540
And I think that's
going to be a new model.

00:20:07.540 --> 00:20:09.470
[INAUDIBLE] a different
way of thinking.

00:20:09.470 --> 00:20:11.590
I tend to think that
people will change

00:20:11.590 --> 00:20:13.015
as they grow with technology.

00:20:13.015 --> 00:20:14.890
The way people think
about things is probably

00:20:14.890 --> 00:20:16.450
different than the way I
used to think about them when

00:20:16.450 --> 00:20:17.570
I was young.

00:20:17.570 --> 00:20:19.980
Because just technology
is there and ever present.

00:20:19.980 --> 00:20:22.820
And I think live streaming
seems to be a really big deal

00:20:22.820 --> 00:20:23.750
for people nowadays.

00:20:23.750 --> 00:20:24.800
MELANIE WARRICK: Well,
Mark is doing that.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:26.070
And so is Holden,
actually, at this time.

00:20:26.070 --> 00:20:26.260
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:20:26.260 --> 00:20:26.800
There's a few of us.

00:20:26.800 --> 00:20:27.290
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:20:27.290 --> 00:20:27.870
MANDY WAITE: That's great.

00:20:27.870 --> 00:20:29.090
MARK MANDEL: Chris
[INAUDIBLE] on the Go team.

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:29.091
[INAUDIBLE]

00:20:29.091 --> 00:20:29.790
MELANIE WARRICK: Yeah.

00:20:29.790 --> 00:20:30.220
MANDY WAITE: Excellent.

00:20:30.220 --> 00:20:30.310
Yeah.

00:20:30.310 --> 00:20:31.500
So live stream is a new one.

00:20:31.500 --> 00:20:32.690
So these are the
things we wouldn't

00:20:32.690 --> 00:20:33.890
have predicted a while back.

00:20:33.890 --> 00:20:35.348
I'm hoping that
next year, there'll

00:20:35.348 --> 00:20:37.970
be another really great
innovative way of actually

00:20:37.970 --> 00:20:39.620
doing this kind of
developer relations

00:20:39.620 --> 00:20:40.910
and getting the
message out to people.

00:20:40.910 --> 00:20:42.200
MELANIE WARRICK: Maybe
something with IoT.

00:20:42.200 --> 00:20:42.950
MARK MANDEL: Yeah.

00:20:42.950 --> 00:20:44.264
So on a practical note--

00:20:44.264 --> 00:20:45.930
and you were talking
about this as well,

00:20:45.930 --> 00:20:47.532
how tailoring the
DevRel experience

00:20:47.532 --> 00:20:49.490
for different communities
and different groups.

00:20:49.490 --> 00:20:51.560
You run the Infrastructure
and Ops group, which

00:20:51.560 --> 00:20:53.870
is a very different community,
say, than maybe the wider tech

00:20:53.870 --> 00:20:54.370
community.

00:20:54.370 --> 00:20:57.549
How have you seen basically
the approach for DevRel

00:20:57.549 --> 00:20:59.840
in that space be different
from, say, other communities

00:20:59.840 --> 00:21:00.890
that you've worked in?

00:21:00.890 --> 00:21:03.680
MANDY WAITE: I think the DevOps
community have a different need

00:21:03.680 --> 00:21:06.440
from the more general
developer community.

00:21:06.440 --> 00:21:08.085
I kind of struggle
with saying that

00:21:08.085 --> 00:21:10.460
at times, because I think
we've grown up with developers.

00:21:10.460 --> 00:21:11.834
We've been in
developer relations

00:21:11.834 --> 00:21:13.930
for 10 years, 11 years or so.

00:21:13.930 --> 00:21:17.047
And it's easy to think that
developers and DevOps are

00:21:17.047 --> 00:21:19.130
different fundamentally,
that the way we should do

00:21:19.130 --> 00:21:21.110
messaging to them is different.

00:21:21.110 --> 00:21:24.140
I think, at the moment, we don't
have very many people doing

00:21:24.140 --> 00:21:26.110
developer relations for DevOps.

00:21:26.110 --> 00:21:28.280
And so I think the
key here is to look

00:21:28.280 --> 00:21:31.040
to hire people who are
practitioners, people who

00:21:31.040 --> 00:21:32.860
actually come from
that community,

00:21:32.860 --> 00:21:35.770
are part of that community,
and bring them onto the team,

00:21:35.770 --> 00:21:38.910
and have them advocate on
behalf of that community

00:21:38.910 --> 00:21:41.432
to our product teams, and
also understand their needs

00:21:41.432 --> 00:21:42.890
and help them solve
their problems,

00:21:42.890 --> 00:21:44.594
because the problems
that are community

00:21:44.594 --> 00:21:46.010
have are exactly
the same problems

00:21:46.010 --> 00:21:48.062
that they've had up till now.

00:21:48.062 --> 00:21:50.020
And so I think that's
really, really important.

00:21:50.020 --> 00:21:51.830
And I'm going to
follow up on that.

00:21:51.830 --> 00:21:54.770
I'm just going to just break
the question a little bit.

00:21:54.770 --> 00:21:57.140
I often find myself
drawing to people

00:21:57.140 --> 00:21:59.750
on a whiteboard something
called a Treynor curve.

00:21:59.750 --> 00:22:01.570
So Ben Treynor has this curve.

00:22:01.570 --> 00:22:04.890
Ben Treynor is the guy who
invented SRE at Google.

00:22:04.890 --> 00:22:07.310
And we have this Treynor curve
about how people's skills

00:22:07.310 --> 00:22:08.180
change over time.

00:22:08.180 --> 00:22:10.100
And I constantly
draw this picture

00:22:10.100 --> 00:22:13.000
whereby a practitioner would
have core practitioners skills,

00:22:13.000 --> 00:22:15.712
and maybe not so much advocacy
skills at the time they start,

00:22:15.712 --> 00:22:18.170
and that their practitioner
skills would weigh in over time

00:22:18.170 --> 00:22:20.010
and their advocacy
skills would improve.

00:22:20.010 --> 00:22:21.590
And so I have my
own Treynor curve

00:22:21.590 --> 00:22:24.790
whereby advocacy skills are now
much better than practitioner

00:22:24.790 --> 00:22:25.290
skills.

00:22:25.290 --> 00:22:27.620
And I think, at times,
you need to kick energy

00:22:27.620 --> 00:22:29.780
back into that to say,
hey, you need to go back

00:22:29.780 --> 00:22:31.160
to being a practitioner again.

00:22:31.160 --> 00:22:32.716
It may well be that they
feel comfortable in it.

00:22:32.716 --> 00:22:35.257
They're really on it, and they
actually know what's going on.

00:22:35.257 --> 00:22:36.950
But I think from
time to time, you

00:22:36.950 --> 00:22:39.410
need to step back and say, OK,
what is my community doing?

00:22:39.410 --> 00:22:41.326
Let me go back and be
one of them for a while.

00:22:41.326 --> 00:22:43.010
And I'd like to see
us do more of that

00:22:43.010 --> 00:22:44.450
when it comes to practitioners.

00:22:44.450 --> 00:22:45.760
So I think that's
really, really helpful--

00:22:45.760 --> 00:22:48.350
having practitioners and keeping
those practitioner skills.

00:22:48.350 --> 00:22:51.800
And I think it's also possible
to pivot to a practitioner role

00:22:51.800 --> 00:22:52.340
as well.

00:22:52.340 --> 00:22:54.050
And I think we have an
evidence of that on my team

00:22:54.050 --> 00:22:55.830
currently when it
comes to security,

00:22:55.830 --> 00:22:57.594
which Jane is doing as well.

00:22:57.594 --> 00:22:59.510
And I think that's really,
really interesting.

00:22:59.510 --> 00:23:04.292
I think there's also a lot
of space for more generalists

00:23:04.292 --> 00:23:05.750
to grow up into
that space as well,

00:23:05.750 --> 00:23:07.500
and to learn from
the practitioners,

00:23:07.500 --> 00:23:09.000
and to become
practitioners as well.

00:23:09.000 --> 00:23:11.060
So having more senior
people as practitioners,

00:23:11.060 --> 00:23:14.360
but having more junior
people as the people who

00:23:14.360 --> 00:23:17.306
learn from them, take their
messages on the road, maybe not

00:23:17.306 --> 00:23:18.680
have the same kind
of recognition

00:23:18.680 --> 00:23:21.260
as they do, but can help
with content, and videos,

00:23:21.260 --> 00:23:21.890
and such like.

00:23:21.890 --> 00:23:24.098
And I think those people
are really, really key to it

00:23:24.098 --> 00:23:26.390
as well, because there are
not that many practitioners

00:23:26.390 --> 00:23:27.060
in the world.

00:23:27.060 --> 00:23:28.710
And so if you want to have
a reasonably large team

00:23:28.710 --> 00:23:30.410
and good coverage,
then you're probably

00:23:30.410 --> 00:23:32.951
not going to be able to do that
just by hiring practitioners.

00:23:32.951 --> 00:23:34.970
So the other thing to
get from practitioners--

00:23:34.970 --> 00:23:37.070
I had this conversation
with [INAUDIBLE] recently

00:23:37.070 --> 00:23:38.610
about trust versus credibility.

00:23:38.610 --> 00:23:39.590
And I do agree.

00:23:39.590 --> 00:23:41.040
Trust is extremely important.

00:23:41.040 --> 00:23:43.541
We need people to
trust our advocates

00:23:43.541 --> 00:23:44.540
when they talk to them--

00:23:44.540 --> 00:23:46.774
I know this person, I trust
what they're telling me.

00:23:46.774 --> 00:23:48.940
And at the same time, our
Product Team stakeholders,

00:23:48.940 --> 00:23:50.720
as well, should trust
them and trust them

00:23:50.720 --> 00:23:53.000
when they bring stories
and feedback back

00:23:53.000 --> 00:23:54.677
to the product teams
that say, maybe

00:23:54.677 --> 00:23:55.760
you should do it this way.

00:23:55.760 --> 00:23:58.700
They trust that person's
view, and they give it

00:23:58.700 --> 00:23:59.840
due consideration.

00:23:59.840 --> 00:24:01.715
MELANIE WARRICK: I know
trust, in particular,

00:24:01.715 --> 00:24:04.230
is very valuable when you
can give them a perspective,

00:24:04.230 --> 00:24:06.730
and it's not necessarily being
driven by the product itself.

00:24:06.730 --> 00:24:09.060
It's being
specifically driven by,

00:24:09.060 --> 00:24:10.560
this is what I know
about the space.

00:24:10.560 --> 00:24:13.060
This is what I'm able to bring
to the table about the space.

00:24:13.060 --> 00:24:14.000
Yeah.

00:24:14.000 --> 00:24:16.345
So when I was asking earlier
before about developer

00:24:16.345 --> 00:24:17.720
relations at Google
and what that

00:24:17.720 --> 00:24:20.540
looked like, a little part of
why I had led down the path

00:24:20.540 --> 00:24:24.710
to that question was I hear
"develoepr evangelists"

00:24:24.710 --> 00:24:26.690
as a term that's
constantly intermixed

00:24:26.690 --> 00:24:27.800
with "developer advocate."

00:24:27.800 --> 00:24:29.540
So I'm curious what
your perspective is

00:24:29.540 --> 00:24:31.900
on these two terms,
and how they relate,

00:24:31.900 --> 00:24:33.230
and how they don't relate.

00:24:33.230 --> 00:24:33.500
MANDY WAITE: Yeah.

00:24:33.500 --> 00:24:35.000
Another really
interesting question.

00:24:35.000 --> 00:24:36.382
I think we're a large company.

00:24:36.382 --> 00:24:38.090
We have a large
developer relations team.

00:24:38.090 --> 00:24:40.880
It's very easy for us to kind of
be very specific about what we

00:24:40.880 --> 00:24:42.920
do when it comes to our role.

00:24:42.920 --> 00:24:45.090
We can shape the role to
be what it needs to be.

00:24:45.090 --> 00:24:47.480
And so let's go back
a bit, take one step.

00:24:47.480 --> 00:24:49.790
It's bidirectional,
our role as advocates.

00:24:49.790 --> 00:24:52.639
It's doing evangelism
effectively, talking to people.

00:24:52.639 --> 00:24:54.680
What I think it's a
different kind of evangelism.

00:24:54.680 --> 00:24:56.000
It's much more like
sitting down with people

00:24:56.000 --> 00:24:58.190
and holding their hands and
talking them through things,

00:24:58.190 --> 00:25:00.650
learning from them and having
them learn from you as you

00:25:00.650 --> 00:25:02.390
talk them through how
you can potentially

00:25:02.390 --> 00:25:03.279
solve their problems.

00:25:03.279 --> 00:25:04.820
At that point, you
might learn things

00:25:04.820 --> 00:25:06.750
that you didn't know about
the product and how it works,

00:25:06.750 --> 00:25:09.390
and which you can bring feedback
back to the product teams.

00:25:09.390 --> 00:25:11.270
So it's not really
fundamentally evangelism,

00:25:11.270 --> 00:25:12.644
although there is a lot of that.

00:25:12.644 --> 00:25:14.060
Going to events
and doing outreach

00:25:14.060 --> 00:25:15.885
is very much a
form of evangelism.

00:25:15.885 --> 00:25:17.510
And at the other end,
there's this kind

00:25:17.510 --> 00:25:19.430
of influence on the product,
and bringing feedback back,

00:25:19.430 --> 00:25:22.130
and making cases, writing what
we call friction logs, which

00:25:22.130 --> 00:25:24.472
are things that detail
somebody's interactions

00:25:24.472 --> 00:25:26.180
with our product and
some of the problems

00:25:26.180 --> 00:25:29.270
that they may have had,
raising bugs, driving fixes,

00:25:29.270 --> 00:25:31.490
showing people
what kind of impact

00:25:31.490 --> 00:25:35.690
is likely to be achieved through
addressing a particular issue.

00:25:35.690 --> 00:25:38.280
And I think evangelism
is one half of that.

00:25:38.280 --> 00:25:41.000
And I think some teams,
some smaller DevOps teams,

00:25:41.000 --> 00:25:43.430
can't really afford to
do both parts of it.

00:25:43.430 --> 00:25:45.740
It's much more about
getting the message out,

00:25:45.740 --> 00:25:47.660
and so they focus
more specifically

00:25:47.660 --> 00:25:49.700
on the evangelism
component of it.

00:25:49.700 --> 00:25:53.090
But I think advocacy is rooted
in that bidirectional thing

00:25:53.090 --> 00:25:54.706
whereby we influence
the product teams

00:25:54.706 --> 00:25:56.080
and we influence
our communities.

00:25:56.080 --> 00:25:57.230
Did that answer your
question [INAUDIBLE]??

00:25:57.230 --> 00:25:58.896
MELANIE WARRICK: Yes,
it does, actually.

00:25:58.896 --> 00:26:01.310
MARK MANDEL: I'm curious,
also, to know your thoughts on,

00:26:01.310 --> 00:26:03.089
if people listen
to this podcast,

00:26:03.089 --> 00:26:05.630
or they've heard other things
about developer relations, what

00:26:05.630 --> 00:26:06.530
have been some paths--

00:26:06.530 --> 00:26:07.730
or are there paths
that you think

00:26:07.730 --> 00:26:09.920
would be great for people
who want to get involved

00:26:09.920 --> 00:26:11.360
with developer relations?

00:26:11.360 --> 00:26:13.970
Because it seems like a fairly
niche thing in some ways.

00:26:13.970 --> 00:26:14.720
MANDY WAITE: Yeah.

00:26:14.720 --> 00:26:17.461
Really interesting insight,
because the practitioner way

00:26:17.461 --> 00:26:18.960
of thinking about
it is interesting.

00:26:18.960 --> 00:26:21.020
I mean, obviously, we're
hiring practitioners.

00:26:21.020 --> 00:26:22.475
Then they may not
come necessarily

00:26:22.475 --> 00:26:24.200
with advocacy
skills, but they're

00:26:24.200 --> 00:26:26.630
going to want to want
to do that kind of work.

00:26:26.630 --> 00:26:28.640
And when you interview
people and you ask them

00:26:28.640 --> 00:26:30.056
why develop
relations, it's always

00:26:30.056 --> 00:26:33.080
about showing people
how things work.

00:26:33.080 --> 00:26:34.370
I like teaching people.

00:26:34.370 --> 00:26:36.135
I've always been like that.

00:26:36.135 --> 00:26:37.760
You find that you
look at their GitHub,

00:26:37.760 --> 00:26:39.560
and it's all based
on tutorials and such

00:26:39.560 --> 00:26:41.300
like where they've been
producing tutorials to show

00:26:41.300 --> 00:26:42.300
people how to do things.

00:26:42.300 --> 00:26:43.954
And it's really interesting.

00:26:43.954 --> 00:26:45.620
Some people have the
mindset that I just

00:26:45.620 --> 00:26:46.475
want to help people.

00:26:46.475 --> 00:26:50.570
Some people, also, once they've
experienced pain in the past--

00:26:50.570 --> 00:26:52.790
and I don't mean physical
pain, but the pain

00:26:52.790 --> 00:26:54.680
that I experienced
at Sun Microsystems

00:26:54.680 --> 00:26:56.300
when I was trying
to make something

00:26:56.300 --> 00:26:59.840
called OpenSolaris, which was an
open source version of Solaris,

00:26:59.840 --> 00:27:02.634
work with all of the Linux
applications and RPMs

00:27:02.634 --> 00:27:04.050
and such like that
were out there.

00:27:04.050 --> 00:27:05.880
And nothing ever worked.

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:07.640
I would spend a lot
of my time googling

00:27:07.640 --> 00:27:10.310
for esoteric error messages.

00:27:10.310 --> 00:27:13.580
And nobody's ever seen
this problem before, ever.

00:27:13.580 --> 00:27:16.307
So I felt very much that I
wanted to blog about that.

00:27:16.307 --> 00:27:17.890
And I wanted to share
that with people

00:27:17.890 --> 00:27:19.014
so that they could find it.

00:27:19.014 --> 00:27:20.759
If they ever hit that
same problem again,

00:27:20.759 --> 00:27:22.050
they would be able to solve it.

00:27:22.050 --> 00:27:24.146
And every time that
happened, I blogged about it,

00:27:24.146 --> 00:27:25.770
I blogged about it,
I blogged about it.

00:27:25.770 --> 00:27:27.980
And I think that's something
that a lot of people

00:27:27.980 --> 00:27:29.240
like doing, is like
they don't want

00:27:29.240 --> 00:27:30.710
people to experience
the same kind of pain

00:27:30.710 --> 00:27:31.751
that they've experienced.

00:27:31.751 --> 00:27:34.300
And so that's another reason
to come into this kind of role.

00:27:34.300 --> 00:27:36.050
You want to make the
world a better place.

00:27:36.050 --> 00:27:39.540
I used to have my tagline for
Twitter was, make the cloud

00:27:39.540 --> 00:27:41.700
a better place for developers.

00:27:41.700 --> 00:27:43.740
So I think some people
just want to do that.

00:27:43.740 --> 00:27:46.160
The question was more about
paths into advocacy, right?

00:27:46.160 --> 00:27:48.290
But I think you need
that part of it.

00:27:48.290 --> 00:27:49.730
You need that mentality.

00:27:49.730 --> 00:27:51.080
You've seen the best speakers.

00:27:51.080 --> 00:27:51.980
You know, they're the
ones that actually

00:27:51.980 --> 00:27:54.514
really care and do have
empathy for their audience.

00:27:54.514 --> 00:27:56.180
And that's always
going to be the case--

00:27:56.180 --> 00:27:58.600
having empathy, not
developing necessarily

00:27:58.600 --> 00:27:59.864
all that is possible.

00:27:59.864 --> 00:28:01.280
Having empathy for
your developers

00:28:01.280 --> 00:28:03.336
is also key-- or
your target audience.

00:28:03.336 --> 00:28:05.210
I'm overloading that
word "developers" again,

00:28:05.210 --> 00:28:07.100
which happens all the
time here currently.

00:28:07.100 --> 00:28:08.641
MELANIE WARRICK:
Where would you want

00:28:08.641 --> 00:28:10.220
to see this field grow into?

00:28:10.220 --> 00:28:12.500
How do you want to
see it evolve, based

00:28:12.500 --> 00:28:13.627
on what you've seen so far?

00:28:13.627 --> 00:28:15.960
MANDY WAITE: Yeah, that's a
really interesting question.

00:28:15.960 --> 00:28:17.550
I'll have to give
that some thought.

00:28:17.550 --> 00:28:19.520
I want it to be bigger.

00:28:19.520 --> 00:28:21.080
I like the fact
that it's different,

00:28:21.080 --> 00:28:22.850
that people do it differently
and people approach it

00:28:22.850 --> 00:28:23.349
differently.

00:28:23.349 --> 00:28:25.430
I like it to be
continuously evolving.

00:28:25.430 --> 00:28:29.150
I don't think there's enough
standardization around it.

00:28:29.150 --> 00:28:32.420
I would like to see it moving
away from being a marketing

00:28:32.420 --> 00:28:33.890
function, which it often is.

00:28:33.890 --> 00:28:37.550
I don't think that that's
the best place for DevRel.

00:28:37.550 --> 00:28:39.590
I would much rather
see it rooted

00:28:39.590 --> 00:28:41.210
in engineering principles.

00:28:41.210 --> 00:28:42.747
I know that's not
always possible.

00:28:42.747 --> 00:28:45.080
I'm not sure if I have a
strong opinion of that, though.

00:28:45.080 --> 00:28:47.080
I don't know if I have a
strong opinion of where

00:28:47.080 --> 00:28:47.994
I want it to go.

00:28:47.994 --> 00:28:49.160
I want us to have influence.

00:28:49.160 --> 00:28:50.910
And I want, really,
effectively, the world

00:28:50.910 --> 00:28:53.420
to be a better place for
people who are building tech

00:28:53.420 --> 00:28:54.680
or doing tech.

00:28:54.680 --> 00:28:57.830
And I think tech is
on a path that makes

00:28:57.830 --> 00:28:59.420
it very hard for us to keep up.

00:28:59.420 --> 00:29:00.628
It's likely to be asymptotic.

00:29:00.628 --> 00:29:03.092
The closer we get to having
really good experiences

00:29:03.092 --> 00:29:04.550
for people using
tech is probably--

00:29:04.550 --> 00:29:07.551
the further away from us it
gets makes it more difficult.

00:29:07.551 --> 00:29:09.800
MELANIE WARRICK: Through
your experience, your history

00:29:09.800 --> 00:29:12.800
of working in tech, I'm just
curious as a side thing, what

00:29:12.800 --> 00:29:15.530
is one of the technologies that
you've seen that really you

00:29:15.530 --> 00:29:17.540
were so excited about
when it finally came,

00:29:17.540 --> 00:29:19.850
and you felt like it
completely had this huge impact

00:29:19.850 --> 00:29:21.710
that you wouldn't have
imagined or expected?

00:29:21.710 --> 00:29:22.520
MANDY WAITE: Yeah,
I think I've already

00:29:22.520 --> 00:29:24.697
mentioned it-- container
scheduling communities.

00:29:24.697 --> 00:29:25.530
MELANIE WARRICK: Oh.

00:29:25.530 --> 00:29:26.571
MANDY WAITE: Docker was--

00:29:26.571 --> 00:29:29.120
I watched Solomon Hykes
giving a talk at dotScale--

00:29:29.120 --> 00:29:32.000
which is happening in Paris
next week, and I will there--

00:29:32.000 --> 00:29:34.604
watching Solomon Hykes
talk about Docker in 2013

00:29:34.604 --> 00:29:35.770
was really, really exciting.

00:29:35.770 --> 00:29:38.200
And the comparison to
containers, shipping containers

00:29:38.200 --> 00:29:40.430
and such like, it
just really moved me.

00:29:40.430 --> 00:29:41.430
It was really fantastic.

00:29:41.430 --> 00:29:42.710
It was great event, actually.

00:29:42.710 --> 00:29:46.130
And having containers
was great, but having

00:29:46.130 --> 00:29:48.700
that ability to schedule them,
like the way we do at Google,

00:29:48.700 --> 00:29:50.130
was even better for me.

00:29:50.130 --> 00:29:51.787
It was, like, I really get this.

00:29:51.787 --> 00:29:53.370
I really do understand
how this works.

00:29:53.370 --> 00:29:54.940
And I really want to
make this a thing.

00:29:54.940 --> 00:29:56.820
I became very determined
to make it a thing.

00:29:56.820 --> 00:29:59.584
And hopefully, I made some
small contribution to that.

00:29:59.584 --> 00:30:00.500
MARK MANDEL: I'm sure.

00:30:00.500 --> 00:30:01.280
MELANIE WARRICK: I know you did.

00:30:01.280 --> 00:30:02.740
MANDY WAITE: Kubernetes
was very much

00:30:02.740 --> 00:30:04.280
the thing that made me
most excited-- these kind

00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:05.460
of paradigm shifts.

00:30:05.460 --> 00:30:07.280
Although on the
whole, I probably

00:30:07.280 --> 00:30:08.960
would say cloud-native
generally.

00:30:08.960 --> 00:30:10.640
The whole principles
behind cloud-native

00:30:10.640 --> 00:30:13.790
are probably more important
as Kubernetes becomes

00:30:13.790 --> 00:30:16.270
more of the new infrastructure
that kind of bubbles down

00:30:16.270 --> 00:30:19.070
into the substrate, as I used
to describe it to people.

00:30:19.070 --> 00:30:20.640
And things get
built on top of it,

00:30:20.640 --> 00:30:22.740
because it's an
enabler for new things.

00:30:22.740 --> 00:30:25.290
I think we can do services
better now that we have things

00:30:25.290 --> 00:30:27.050
like Istio and other
things like that that

00:30:27.050 --> 00:30:28.841
are coming on-- service
messages generally.

00:30:28.841 --> 00:30:31.005
And all of the [INAUDIBLE]
system components

00:30:31.005 --> 00:30:32.550
that are coming up
around Kubernetes

00:30:32.550 --> 00:30:34.170
is just really, really exciting.

00:30:34.170 --> 00:30:36.460
And I think it just changes
the way we think about it.

00:30:36.460 --> 00:30:38.626
I'd love to get away from
traditional infrastructure

00:30:38.626 --> 00:30:40.460
completely and just
be using containers

00:30:40.460 --> 00:30:41.942
and not worry about
where they run.

00:30:41.942 --> 00:30:42.900
